From 102d4cb33ca12e4f456d1fd7e85c37a12d3e8c44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 20:49:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08100/11362] The agent produces exactly one positive example
 of winning, then hides it from the prompt that asks

`next_move` appends the level-up transition to `self.transitions`, and only THEN calls
`_observe_level_boundary` -> `_begin_level_goal_episode`, which sets
`_episode_transition_start = len(self.transitions)` -- one PAST the row just appended.
`_active_transitions()` slices from that index, and EVERY live induction prompt is built from it.
So the single positive example the agent ever produces for itself -- the transition whose action
incremented the level counter -- was unreachable from the prompt asking what winning looks like.

Measured 2026-08-01 over 138 induced engines from 21 games: 93 are live and 71 of those fail on
the GOAL rather than the dynamics. Reading all 71, 34 are an unconditional `return False` and 3
more never return. Across every rebuilt live prompt, `_transitions_block`'s WIN TRANSITION block
fired ZERO times. The consumer was correct and starved: its own comments record that `next_grid`
is the next level's opening board and `win.grid` is one action short, so it deliberately emits the
labelled transition EVENT instead. It just never received one.

WHY NOT MOVE THE START INDEX BACK BY ONE, the obvious fix, which is wrong. The completing action
ALSO re-lays out the playfield for the next level -- 3527 of 4096 cells on ka59 against an
ordinary-step median of 18.5. Leaving it in the NEW level's dynamics window would teach the
proposer that one action can change 86% of the board, trading a starved goal prompt for a
corrupted dynamics prompt. The transition is carried BESIDE the window: captured before the index
moves, passed as its own `win_transition` parameter, never merged into `trans`.

`_transitions_block` keeps its historical scan of `trans` as the fallback, so a supplied
transition wins and nothing else changes. PROVEN, not asserted: `induce_prompt` output is
BYTE-IDENTICAL to the renderer recovered from git on all six captured games.

THE TEST PINS THE DEFECT, NOT THE REMEDY -- it accepts any route that makes the win transition
reachable. Three companion tests matter as much: the exclusion reproduces in the LIVE ordering
(append, then observe); the consumer exists and is careful; and `_previous_level_complete_grid`
must NOT be mistaken for the positive example -- it is the next level's opening board, and
treating it as a win state is the "win-state poison" corrected on 2026-07-29.

FRESHNESS: 13 artifacts acknowledged, evidence graded rather than uniform. experiment_6011 carries
PROVEN BY REBUILD (its rebuild_command was run; ZERO substantive movement, all 20 differences
being provenance bookkeeping a rebuild deletes). The rest carry the weaker BYTE-IDENTITY argument
and say so: the differential over the remaining five was stopped mid-flight because its
restore-from-snapshot step would have wiped the acknowledgements being written -- a race lost
deliberately rather than silently. Two are CASCADES, staled only because an upstream artifact's
bytes changed when an acknowledgement was appended to it. Every results/ diff is additive; two
show a `-1` from a long string re-wrapping and were verified leaf-by-leaf as LOST=0 CHANGED=0.

MUTATION MARKER RETIRED DELIBERATELY, per the gate's own instructions rather than by reverting.
The marker was armed by a CONCURRENT session's pytest run, and both paths it flagged
(experiment_6012, gateway_accurate_rescore) were marked `[NOT this run]` -- the guard did not
observe that run writing them, because they are MINE: the two acknowledgements above, +16 -0 each,
adding only `freshness_acknowledgements` keys. The marker protected nothing else. `git checkout --`
on that list, which the gate offers first, would have destroyed authored work -- the failure mode
its own CAUTION names as having happened three times.

SCOPE, HONESTLY. This makes the win block ABLE to fire on the live path. It does not show that a
model given one positive example writes a better predicate -- unmeasured. It does not touch the
52% `return False` slice, which is a DETECTION problem an example cannot fix. And the offline
harnesses starve the same block by a different route: their split puts the level-up row in the
HELD-OUT half ("1 in the window, 0 shown, 1 held out, every game"). Untouched here.

Banked levels remain 3/3.

